WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.624
Step 1: get traces in front of a person.

2
00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:04.141
Start by sampling.

3
00:00:04.421 --> 00:00:11.653
The FAQ suggests beginning with roughly 100 diverse traces and annotating at least the first 30 yourself.

4
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:21.107
“Diverse” matters more than random: make sure the sample covers your main features, user types, and channels, and include traces users complained about.

5
00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:24.821
If you only sample the happy path, you’ll learn about the happy path.

6
00:00:25.101 --> 00:00:26.509
Next, remove friction.

7
00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:40.613
Husain’s field guide calls a simple custom data viewer one of the most valuable investments a team can make: everything about one trace on one screen, a quick pass or fail, a free-text note, and hotkeys to move on.

8
00:00:40.893 --> 00:00:47.123
It also says a spreadsheet beats nothing, and for a first pass of a hundred traces a spreadsheet is fine.

9
00:00:47.403 --> 00:00:50.581
The lab gives you a CSV template for exactly this.

10
00:00:50.861 --> 00:00:54.381
Then judge each trace with a binary label, pass or fail.

11
00:00:54.661 --> 00:01:07.632
The FAQ recommends binary judgments over 1 to 5 scales because they force a decision, make labels more consistent between sessions and people, and avoid the drift toward the middle that rating scales invite.

12
00:01:07.912 --> 00:01:13.501
If you want to track gradual progress, use several specific binary checks rather than one scale.
